  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Founding
    
    A Nightshelf merchant guild formed at The Drowned Ledge, the deep-mining outpost whose catastrophic flooding is officially recorded as geological accident. Night-Assessor Cevmucsiatum-larv~ and the trader ☽'tenebris judged that the Flipper Accord — the trade agreement binding Aurora Stack and Nightshelf economies — left exploitable margins in fire-core distribution. The Collective was built to move fire-cores without Aurora oversight, selling to buyers who paid without requiring Accord-sanctioned documentation. Its founding principle was that hoarded knowledge constitutes preserved power, a conviction closely aligned with Mandatum∴absconditu, the Nightshelf doctrine that survival demands secrecy.
    
    The relationship between the Collective and The Dreaming Tooth — the orca-origin artifact held by the Wake-Singers at the Corpse Current — appears in the custodial record but is not explained by any source I have located. The formal designation is *owned_by*. I will not speculate on what this means. I will note that no one has offered me an explanation, and that I have asked.
    
    ## Commercial Operations
    
    The Collective operates from Sleek☽ Caemomot, its deep-tunnel trading hub, and controls the Thrall☽ Vavmastius corridor, ☽'shadow Umbrsulcavus~ (an extraction site), and The Forgotten Shore. Merchant-runners carry fire-amulets and bone-clasps recognized at checkpoints between The Drowned Ledge and the mid-tunnels.
    
    Fire-core inventory is graded by class and taint-certified per Nightshelf assay before receiving ☽'tenebris's triple-mark seal:
    
    | Class | Price (aurora-shard equivalent) | Notes |
    |---|---|---|
    | II | 43 shards | Standard grade |
    | III | 61 shards | Purple-spectrum; reportedly burns twice as long as surface-logged alternatives |
    | IV | By consultation only | — |
    
    Beyond the Collective's own stock, ☽'tenebris offers tripartite certification for other merchants' goods — her seal applied to cores she did not source. All transactions settle in fire-crystal or aurora-shard equivalent; surface scrip and Accord-logged tender are refused. The Pactum∴ne — not the Flipper Accord — governs every Collective contract. Deep-seam relics of uncertain provenance also move through their networks, sold as-found with cipher documentation, decryption keys furnished only upon completed payment.
    
    An estimated 60–65% of total Coven commerce bypasses Accord-sanctioned trade, moving through at least three confirmed tunnel routes that avoid the Courier Corps. Official manifests record as few as nine Coven-stamped crates per cycle at Midway Shelf. The gap between those nine crates and the Collective's actual volume is the entry's most important number, and the one no official source acknowledges.
    
    ## Institutional Decline
    
    The Icebound Exchange, Aurora Stack's dominant merchant guild, has systematically absorbed routes the Collective once controlled, a process the Exchange terms *market consolidation*. The Collective's position at the Veil Vents collapsed when E'frostlings, a surface faction based at the Fourth Terrace, launched a territorial push that broke the Collective's hold before a rival settlement called Threshold also fell — costing the Collective influence over twelve dependent guilds. E'frostlings field assessors have since profiled the Collective's operations at ☽'shadow Umbrsulcavus~, documenting a 31% extraction-output decline and accelerating corruption spreading from The Forgotten Shore into mining chambers. Fire-core shipments from the site have arrived short of manifest, with courier schedules from the upper tunnels growing erratic.
    
    The Shadow Parliament, the Collective's governing body, has not convened in full quorum for two cycles. Leadership calls this *restructuring*. Umbra-Princeps Alnae~ Oss, founder of the allied ☽'shadow Covens' Order, judged more bluntly that the institution had forgotten its founding purpose — "its secrecy calcified into spite wearing the mask of devotion." Sentries posted beneath Terrace Momiou to guard sealed fire-core caches showed signs of being Touched: murmuring to empty air, awareness hollowed, devotion to their posts intact. The corruption source was traced to a buried fire-core feeding taint through the Order's ranks.
    
    The Collective's entire commercial charter depends on the Swaleed~ Luminary, a fire-scarred lantern whose flame authenticates the Pactum∴silenti. If the Luminary's glass breaks, the legal basis for every standing Collective contract is extinguished with it.
    
    ## Political Maneuvering
    
    The Collective adapted during the Orca Incursion by allying with the Communion, distributing Star∴eternal — a communion-sourced practice — through its trade networks. During the Aurora Stack succession crisis following Luminary Meridian-of-the-First-Light's death, fourteen crates of Class III fire-cores bearing ☽'tenebris's triple-mark arrived at Forum Floe through unregistered channels. No ledger was filed. None were refused. This is the kind of intervention that fills a power vacuum without formally entering it — and it is worth noting that an entity nominally allied with the Exchange was simultaneously undermining the Exchange's monopoly on crisis supply. The relationship list records *allied_with*. The manifests record something else.
    
    ## Current Status
    
    The Collective holds what it can: Sleek☽ Caemomot, Thrall☽ Vavmastius, Scar☽ Lucamlinaecius, and trade in goods that leave no ledger. Its members still swear blood-oaths. Some still mean them. Whether the faction survives the current *transition* — whatever the Council means by that — depends on whether hoarded knowledge remains power when the ice around it is melting.
